  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Images of Immigrants and Refugees has already shown clear early reach: it has been downloaded 10,860 times and has 4 citations in OpenAlex. The record also points to policy and social relevance through its alignment with the SDG \u201cReduced inequalities,\u201d while funding support from KU Leuven and the Belgian Federal Science Policy Office suggests institutional backing for the project\u2019s aims.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly book is an open-access publication from Leuven University Press that examines how immigrants and refugees are represented, a topic that sits squarely at the intersection of media studies, migration, political science, and geography. Its strongest impact signal is usage: the book has been downloaded 10,860 times, indicating substantial reader interest and active consultation in the years since publication. The open-access availability likely supports that reach, and the publication record shows multiple open instances.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still modest but meaningful for a 2019 book, with 4 OpenAlex citations spread across 2019, 2020, 2022, and 2023. The citing works are all journal articles, and they appear in venues focused on migration, intercultural communication, ethnic studies, and population research. That pattern suggests the book is being taken up within adjacent research communities concerned with refugees, immigration, media framing, and public opinion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader evidence points to thematic relevance beyond scholarship alone. The book is associated with the SDG \u201cReduced inequalities,\u201d which is consistent with its focus on immigrants and refugees and with the policy-facing nature of the topic. In addition, the funding record names KU Leuven and the Belgian Federal Science Policy Office, indicating that the work was supported by institutions with an interest in research of public and societal value.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library subject classifications further show where the book is being discovered and used: migration and asylum, press and journalism, media studies, and communication industries. Taken together, the record portrays a book with strong usage, a small but growing scholarly footprint, and clear relevance to debates about migration, representation, and inequality. The impact story is therefore early rather than conclusive, but the available signals are coherent and encouraging.</p>",
